// Title: HackTool - PrintSpoofer Execution
// Sigma ID: 8d280471-b4a1-4105-8417-c6edc0c3bf9b
// Status: experimental | Level: high
// Author: Ashish Pulivarthy (ashish-cybersec)
// Date: 2026-07-10 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: privilege-escalation, stealth, T1134.001
// Description: Detects the execution of PrintSpoofer, a privilege escalation tool by @itm4n that abuses the
//   Windows Print Spooler service and named pipe impersonation to escalate from LOCAL/NETWORK
//   SERVICE to SYSTEM using the SeImpersonatePrivilege token privilege. The tool is widely used in
//   red team engagements and post-exploitation scenarios, particularly on IIS or SQL Server service
//   accounts that carry SeImpersonatePrivilege by default. It is effective on Windows 10 and Windows
//   Server 2016/2019.
// False positives:
//   - Unknown
// References:
//   - https://github.com/itm4n/PrintSpoofer
//   - https://itm4n.github.io/printspoofer-abusing-impersonate-privileges/
//   - https://book.hacktricks.xyz/windows-hardening/windows-local-privilege-escalation/roguepotato-and-printspoofer
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/d36f5f09311e4f9c34f951176e7b91e87dbb11b6/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_hktl_printspoofer.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where ((FolderPath endswith "\\PrintSpoofer.exe" or FolderPath endswith "\\PrintSpoofer32.exe" or FolderPath endswith "\\PrintSpoofer64.exe") or ProcessVersionInfoOriginalFileName =~ "PrintSpoofer.exe") or ((ProcessCommandLine contains " -i -c " or ProcessCommandLine contains " -d ") and ProcessCommandLine contains "pipe\\spoolss")
